The '''automotive industry in [[Balboa]]''' is primarily import-driven, with no domestic passenger car manufacturing. The market is supplied by vehicles imported from several countries, with some {{w|completely knocked down|CKD}} (completely knocked down) assembly of passenger vehicles and domestic bodywork and assembly of buses and trucks. The sector has seen rapid growth in {{w|electric vehicle}} adoption, driven by the country's low-cost electricity supply from nuclear and hydroelectric sources and the availability of affordable electric vehicles from [[Balisca]] and [[Burawa]].

== Market overview ==

Balboa's vehicle fleet stands at approximately 5.5 million vehicles (2026), or roughly 156 vehicles per 1,000 people — well below the average for upper-middle-income countries, reflecting the country's high income inequality (Gini coefficient 48.2), the concentration of wealth in [[Estret Province]], and the availability of public transit in major urban areas. Annual new vehicle sales total approximately 95,000 units, while the used vehicle market — dominated by secondary and tertiary purchases in the interior provinces — accounts for an additional approximately 180,000 transactions per year.

The low vehicle ownership rate is a function of both affordability and geography. With a national median household income of approximately $6,500 and a GDP per capita (nominal) of $9,700, the purchase of even a low-cost electric vehicle — priced from approximately $8,000 for an entry-level [[SKQ]] model — represents a significant financial commitment for much of the population. The bottom 40 per cent of earners, who account for only 12 per cent of national income, are largely excluded from vehicle ownership. The middle class — approximately 35 per cent of the population, concentrated in [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Sant Bart]], and [[Novara]] — constitutes the primary market for new vehicle purchases.

== Vehicle ownership by region ==

Vehicle ownership varies dramatically across Balboa's five provinces, reflecting disparities in income, urbanisation, and transit infrastructure:

